dule, but in this case, it\'s used as a loop for the task. Wait, no, \'loop\' is a keyword in Ansible to iterate over items. So perhaps the structure is incorrect because \'loop\' is not properly used.\n\nWait, no, the \'loop\' keyword is used to control how a task is iterated. So perhaps the issue is that the \'loop\' is not properly placed. Let me see: in the task, the \'loop\' is specified at the same level as \'community.crypto...\' which is incorrect. Because \'loop\' is a keyword that affects the entire task, not the module parameters.\n\nWait, no, in Ansible, the \'loop\' keyword is used as a task parameter, not as a module parameter. So the correct structure is:\n\n- name: task name\n  loop: "{{ items }}"\n  module:\n    param: value\n\nWhich is what the faulty playbook has. So that should be correct.\n\nHmm, perhaps the issue is that the \'loop\' variable is not properly defined. But the user said not to change the functional intent, so I shouldn\'t fix that unless it\'s causing the syntax error.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is that the tasks are not indented correctly. Let me check the indentation. Each task starts with \'- name\' at the same level, which is correct. The module and parameters are indented under the task, which is correct.\n\nWait, perhaps the issue is that the playbook is missing a trailing newline. The error message says line 62:38 has no newline at the end of the file. So that\'s an easy fix: add a newline after the last task.\n\nAnother possible issue: in the faulty playbook, the tasks are written with the module and parameters directly under the task, but perhaps the YAML parser expects a different structure. For example, the module and parameters should be a block, not a flow sequence.\n\nWait, looking at the module calls, they are written as block style, which is correct. So that shouldn\'t be the issue.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that the tasks are not properly enclosed within a document. The playbook starts with \'---\' but mayb