content="<think>\nOkay, so I need to create an Ansible Playbook based on the given tasks. Let me go through each task step by step to make sure I understand what's required.\n\nFirst, the playbook must start with '---' on the very first line. That's straightforward. I'll make sure the YAML file begins with that.\n\nNext, the playbook should be valid according to yamllint and ansible-lint standards. I'll have to structure the YAML correctly, using proper indentation and syntax.\n\nThe structure should allow it to be directly included in an Ansible Role under the tasks/ directory, specifically as tasks/main.yml. So, I'll organize the tasks in the correct format without any playbook-level headers like hosts or vars.\n\nNow, looking at the tasks:\n\nTask 1 is to import 'assert.yml'. Since this is for initial assertions and runs once on localhost, I'll use the import_tasks module with the correct parameters. The 'name' should be 'Import assert.yml', and the 'file' should point to 'files/assert.yml'. Also, I'll set 'delegate_to: localhost' and 'register: assertions_result' so the result is captured.\n\nTask 2 is about disabling automatic service management. If 'apt_autostart_state' is 'disabled', I need to create '/usr/sbin/policy-rc.d' with content 'exit 101' and mode 755. I'll use the file module with 'state: present' and 'content: exit 101'. The 'name' should be 'Disable automatic service management'.\n\nTask 3 is the opposite; if 'apt_autostart_state' is 'enabled', I need to remove the policy-rc.d file if it exists. So, I'll use the file module with 'state: absent' and 'name' as 'Enable automatic service management'.\n\nI should ensure that each task is correctly ordered and uses the exact module names and parameters specified. Also, I'll make sure there are no extra comments or explanations, just the YAML tasks.\n\nLet me structure this:\n\n- Start with '---' on the first line.\n- Then, a list of tasks under 'tasks'.\n- Each task has 'name', 'action', and necessary parameters.\n- Use 'import_tasks' for the first task, and 'file' module for the others.\n\nI think that's all. Now, I'll write the YAML accordingly, ensuring proper indentation and structure.\n</think>\n\n```yaml\n---\n- name: Import assert.yml\n  import_tasks:\n    file: files/assert.yml\n  delegate_to: localhost\n  register: assertions_result\n\n- name: Disable automatic service management\n  file:\n    path: /usr/sbin/policy-rc.d\n    state: present\n    content: exit 101\n    mode: '755'\n  when: apt_autostart_state == 'disabled'\n\n- name: Enable automatic service management\n  file:\n    path: /usr/sbin/policy-rc.d\n    state: absent\n  when: apt_autostart_state == 'enabled'\n```" additional_kwargs={} response_metadata={'model': 'deepseek-r1:14b', 'created_at': '2025-10-11T01:36:38.196247558Z', 'done': True, 'done_reason': 'stop', 'total_duration': 10819310870, 'load_duration': 93920960, 'prompt_eval_count': 403, 'prompt_eval_duration': 103715240, 'eval_count': 637, 'eval_duration': 10620488035, 'model_name': 'deepseek-r1:14b'} id='run--31a2761a-7a7c-4d39-ae40-2473c7444dfb-0' usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 403, 'output_tokens': 637, 'total_tokens': 1040}
